  "facts": [
    "Opened in 1848, the High Bridge is the oldest major bridge in New York City, originally serving as a vital part of the Croton Aqueduct, which supplied water to the growing metropolis.",
    "Rising 140 feet above the Harlem River, the High Bridge features a remarkable 450-foot steel arch that replaced five original stone arches between 1926 and 1928, enhancing navigation on the river.",
    "After being closed for decades due to safety concerns, the High Bridge was restored and reopened to pedestrians and cyclists on June 9, 2015, revitalizing this historic landmark."
